  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA well developed nor'easter continues to slowly retrograde off \nthe Mid Atlantic coast, with winds south of southern New England\nalready pushing storm force. The big trend today has been for \nmodels to remain farther southwest than previous runs. Overall\nthis does little to reduce the marine impacts from this system,\nhence why this section is broken out on its own.\n\nPersistent northeast winds have already built seas to a\nconsistent 8 to 12 feet from the central Maine shelf buoy\nsouthward. Combined with periods over 10 seconds and it is an\nideal situation for dangerous nearshore breaking waves and rip\ncurrents. The High Surf Advisory remains in effect thru Sun.\nSeas look to peak sometime late this afternoon or during the \nevening, somewhere around 15 to 17 ft near Jeffrey's Ledge. \n\nBuilding seas and steady northeast wind direction will also be\nideal for generating a large storm surge. It is already sitting\nat 0.75 to 1 ft with plenty of time left to build that thru Sat.\nOur surge forecasts are a little higher than that, so I will\nnudge that down slightly until this afternoon. Overall tides are\nnot their highest of the month, but rising. Given that we need\nat least 2 ft of surge to see flooding at most locations I think\nthis will be a primarily splash-over event. That being said the\nusual vulnerable locations may see minor flooding, and the fact\nthat this occurs over several tide cycles and the dunes may get\nworn down could mean later tides Sun and Mon have a better\nchance at those inundation issues in the trouble spots. The\nCoastal Flood Advisory also remains in effect thru Sun.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe biggest change the shift southwestward has caused is\nreducing the impact of both winds and rainfall across the local\narea.\n\nI still expect an extended period (> 48 hours) of wind gusts\nalong the coast and parts of southern NH greater at or above 35\nmph. With the core of the northeast LLJ shifted so far south\nnow, it is hard to really see much support for widespread 45+\nmph gusts. That is mostly confined to a narrow window this\nafternoon or evening in the vicinity of the Seacoast. Otherwise\nforecast soundings show consistent mixing height tapping the\nbottom of the LLJ around 35 kt. The NBM does taper off\novernight, so my preference is to increase those overnight winds\nbut during the day looks fine. HRRR guidance looks pretty good\nin that respect, so I will blend that in to the NBM guidance.\nStill with leaves left on trees there is a limited power outage\nthreat from falling limbs. \n\nRainfall may be what takes the biggest hit from the shift south.\nSteady rainfall may not make it into most of the forecast area \nuntil late this afternoon or early this evening. So the NBM PoP \nmay have to get delayed a bit again. There is also a noted \ntransition towards more showery or waves of showers as the low \npressure fills and forcing becomes less intense. Ensemble system\nhave all but taken the extreme rainfall scenarios off the \ntable. There are just a handful of members producing amounts \nover 4 inches now. The highest probabilities for a good soaking\nrain (around 1 inch or more) will be south of the mtns, with the\ngreater than 50 percent chance of 2 inches or more confined to\nthe area from Portland to the Lakes Region southwestward into\nthe Monadnocks. The southwest shift also means that it will take\nlonger to clear the region as it moves back northeast\neventually, so the likely PoP and greater has been extended\nfurther into Mon and Mon night.\n\nWarmer and drier weather is expected mid to late week with\ntemperatures a good 5 to 10 degrees above normal.",
